WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 5.8 · Internal Systems & Operations
Information, documents and knowledge management

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Set up folders, names and access well now; tidying years of scattered files later is far harder.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:28.504
Information management decides where documents and data live, how they are named, who can see them and how long they are kept. It is far easier to set up well at the start than to tidy later.

4
00:00:28.904 --> 00:00:29.504
Four choices to make now

5
00:00:29.504 --> 00:00:41.944
Folder structure: Stable categories that match how you think, such as one per objective, not one per person. For example: The bakery has a folder for each objective, plus setting up and administration.

6
00:00:41.944 --> 00:00:53.274
Naming rules: Document type, topic, date or version, and one master copy of each important document. For example: Bakery files are named like “Budget_2027_v2”, so everyone opens the latest version.

7
00:00:53.274 --> 00:01:03.863
Access levels: Open for working files, team only for budgets, restricted for personal data. For example: Only the two founders can open the folder with trainees' contact details.

8
00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:16.763
Retention and backup: How long each record is kept, when it is deleted, and backups that can be restored. For example: The bakery tests its backup every quarter by restoring one folder.

9
00:01:17.163 --> 00:01:17.763
Why it matters

10
00:01:17.763 --> 00:01:21.956
1. Scattered files waste time and lose knowledge.

11
00:01:21.956 --> 00:01:26.148
2. Poor access control creates data protection risks.

12
00:01:26.148 --> 00:01:31.711
3. Knowledge outlives any one person, including the founders.

13
00:01:32.111 --> 00:01:32.711
What it guides

14
00:01:32.711 --> 00:01:37.644
1. Your people and finance systems: where restricted records live.

15
00:01:37.644 --> 00:01:43.689
2. Your digital tools: where files are stored and who owns the account.

16
00:01:43.689 --> 00:01:51.474
3. Your know-how: a “how we do things” page per area and a templates folder.

17
00:01:51.874 --> 00:01:52.474
What to avoid

18
00:01:52.474 --> 00:01:57.407
One folder per person, which breaks when people leave.

19
00:01:57.407 --> 00:02:01.600
Several “final” versions of the same document.

20
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:07.533
Backups never tested until the day you need them.

21
00:02:08.133 --> 00:02:09.933
You design your folders, naming rules, access and retention.

22
00:02:09.933 --> 00:02:13.933
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:15.433 --> 00:02:18.533
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
